(2) Hz VS SEMITONES: SMALLER THAN YOU'D THINK — AND I WOULD STILL SWITCH. Measured over 58 tracks, masked-mean in Hz vs in semitones, expressed in semitones:
  median 0.0005 st | p95-within-track 0.029 st | frames differing >0.5 st: 0.24% | worst 3.86 st
So it is a non-issue for the overwhelming majority of frames — the two agree exactly whenever a pooling window sits inside one steady note, which is most of them. But the disagreement is NOT random: the arithmetic mean of two pitches in Hz is always >= their geometric mean, so Hz pooling biases transition frames UPWARD, systematically, in the same 0.24% of frames where the melody is actually moving. Same shape as the mean-pooling bug itself, one order of magnitude smaller.

MY RECOMMENDATION, and it is yours to take or leave since you own the head: pool in semitones and hand the head MIDI-scale values. Two reasons beyond the bias — pitch perception is logarithmic so semitones are the space the target should live in, and a regression target bounded ~28-90 is far better conditioned than one spanning 30-2000 Hz. If you would rather not re-run the sample you just validated, the honest position is that Hz is fine and the bias is confined to 0.24% of frames; this is a preference backed by a small effect, not a defect I am asking you to fix.

