WHERE I WOULD DIVERGE FROM A PURE PER-FAMILY Z-SCORE: your own numbers show two different phenomena that a single robust score would merge. ptm's 4.49%-over-2.0 is a whole distribution shifted up (median 1.27, p99 2.64, max 5.33) -- everything moved a little. The runaway is a few clips leaving the building (max 163.98 against a 0.88 median). A z-score against the family median catches the second but will also keep flagging the first, just at a recalibrated rate.
I would use TWO tests rather than one: (a) a per-family robust score (median + MAD) for 'this arm is drifting from its own family', and (b) a family-INDEPENDENT hard rail for 'this is broken' -- non-finite, or std beyond something like 10, where no healthy checkpoint of any family has ever sat. 163.98 and 5.33 should not be the same alarm.

THE DISCRIMINATOR I THINK IS MISSING: my 08-10 measurement was PER-CHANNEL, not per-clip -- 166 of 256 channels over 2.0 on the runaway vs 0 of 256 on the healthy arm. That distinction does the separation you want almost for free. A broadly-shifted family moves MOST channels a little; a runaway blows up a SUBSET hard. So 'count of channels over k sigma of that channel's own corpus distribution' should split ptm-baseline from diverging far more cleanly than clip std, which integrates over all 256 and loses the shape. The z0 arrays are already saved next to every clip, so it is a re-read of .latent_sanity.json's inputs, not a re-render.

