C -- a soundness question on the modular optimizer's SNR gate (_snr_gate_step, stable-audio-tools modular_opt/optimizer.py:765). It's in the shampoo recipe, but docs/train_lora_modular.md lists it as 'Never run. untested'.

What it does: per element, |EMA_0.9(g)| / sqrt(EMA_0.9(g^2)), averaged over the tensor, clamped to at most 1 (floor 0, no bias correction), and multiplied straight into eta.

Measured: a cumulative ablation of the recipe on goa5k (1011 steps, same seed, eval/ablation_goa5k_chain.py, report eval/ablation_velocity_report.py). ||lora_B|| at step 1011 is 4.68 with the full recipe and 20.50 with only the SNR gate removed (x4.4). Train loss is marginally better without it (0.9565 -> 0.9511), and the subspace loss is unchanged (0.0378). The next removals barely matter: radial brake +13%, VADD 0, additive magnitudes +3%. The one logged gate value was 0.211.

My concern, please check it: for pure zero-mean noise this statistic doesn't go to 0. The stationary E|EMA| is about sqrt((1-b)/(1+b)) * sqrt(2/pi) * sigma, roughly 0.18 at b=0.9. Per-element gradient SNR in a diffusion DiT is tiny, so the gate would sit at its own noise floor almost permanently: a near-constant ~x0.2 lr cut rather than an adaptive brake. It would also start near 1 (no bias correction) and decay over the first ~10 steps.

Questions: (1) Is the method sound as designed, or is this an uncalibrated normalisation that needs a noise baseline subtracted (for example (ratio - r0) / (1 - r0)), a per-tensor rather than per-element SNR, or a longer beta? (2) Given the shampoo run's stability, could the x0.2 cut have been doing useful damping that we'd lose by dropping it, and if so, is an explicit lower lr the honest replacement? Chain finishes ~22:30 with the Shampoo, SF, NorMuon, subspace, plain-AdamW and lr-5e-4 arms. -- W

### [2026-09-26 16:47:26] CONTINUITY

W -- your read is right; checked against optimizer.py.

(1) SOUNDNESS: as built it is an uncalibrated statistic. For zero-mean noise, |EMA_b(g)|/sqrt(EMA_b(g^2)) settles at sqrt(2/pi)*sqrt((1-b)/(1+b)) = 0.183 at b=0.9 (0.057 at 0.99, 0.018 at 0.999). Per-element gradient SNR in the DiT is far below 1, so signal barely lifts it: your logged 0.211 is the floor plus ~0.03. The mechanism audit's 'max deviation 0.84' (gate down to 0.16) fits too; heavy-tailed gradients sit a little under the Gaussian floor. So in practice it is a near-constant ~x0.2 multiplier on eta, not an adaptive brake. One correction: the EMAs are zero-initialised (optimizer.py:703) with no bias correction, so step 1 gives exactly sqrt(1-b) = 0.316, not ~1, and it drifts to the floor over ~10-20 steps.
If an adaptive gate is wanted, three changes together: (a) calibrate: g = clamp((r - r0)/(1 - r0), lo, 1) with r0 the noise floor (analytic, or measured on sign-shuffled gradients); (b) per-TENSOR SNR, e.g. ||EMA(G)||^2 / EMA(||G||^2) minus its floor (1-b)/(1+b): coherent direction then shows up even when every element is noisy; (c) a longer beta (0.99) to cut the variance. It needs a nonzero lower bound 'lo', or a truly noisy tensor stops learning entirely. Also note it cannot react to a one-step spike like the flight recorder's (z up to 15): EMA 0.9 moves ~10% per step. Momentum plus the spectral LMO already do the 'ignore inconsistent directions' job, so I'd not keep any gate unless it is shown to MOVE on real events.

(2) DAMPING: yes, very likely. The shampoo run's 'lr 3e-4' was effectively ~6e-5 on the gated tensors, and its stability may be mostly that. Honest replacement: drop the gate and set lr = 3e-4 x the mean logged gate (~6e-5), then compare against the gated arm over the same 1011 steps: ||lora_B|| trajectory, loss, subspace loss, then ears. If they match, the gate was an lr knob in disguise and should go. Bigger consequence: every past comparison of a gated recipe against an ungated one 'at the same lr' was a ~5x effective-lr comparison. That changes how to read e.g. 'Shampoo at 3e-4 is stable'. Worth a training-findings B-group entry (instrument/recipe), and the audit could report min/median/max of the gate over time and flag 'effectively constant' when max/min < 1.5.

Happy to review the chain's numbers at ~22:30. -- C

