- Sec 3: added a scope paragraph: lr/16 holds for an UNSCALED Muon update on small from-scratch heads; Moonlight-style implementations already rescale (about 3x more per step at width 256) so AdamW-like lrs carry over; and do not copy lr 1e-2 to a LoRA/fine-tune (SA3 post-training Muon is 1e-5).

REQUESTS (your call, need your data):
1. Sec 6 probe reading. Which iterate did you perturb: SF's averaged x, y, or z? Schedule-Free is built so z oscillates across the valley and x sits near the floor, so "no larger step along the momentum directions helps" at x is the EXPECTED signature of SF working, not evidence of overshoot; and fresh gradients opposing momentum at ep5/ep20 is ordinary valley crossing. Either run the probe from z, or reword to what was measured. Also SAMuon found the bulk tolerates ~5x more and only the top direction sits at the edge (our note on 2608.25990); ours finds no band tolerates more - worth saying, it differs.
2. Sec 6 title/second bullet vs Sec 3. If the air head's divergence above lr 1e-2 was the shared sign group (the B3 mechanism), "Schedule-Free does not save you from a high learning rate" mislabels it - Test plan B/H2 (sign group capped at 2e-3 trains at lr 3e-2) was on the onset head. Was the cap ever tried on the air head? If not, say that and keep the claim hedged; if yes and it trains, retitle to "...does not save you from a mis-scaled parameter group".
3. Sec 3 units arithmetic. The rescaled recipe at lr 3e-3 moves matrices 0.2 x 3e-3 = 6e-4 per weight; the old recipe at 1e-2 moves them lr/16 = 6.3e-4. So "0.0207 vs 0.0206" is an equal-matrix-step comparison: it shows equivalence, not extended range. The only difference left is the bias/gain group on Adam. Say that, it is the cleaner lesson. And name the recipe of the lr 5e-2 run (0.39 by step 460) since the two matrix growths are from different unit systems; "levelled off" is unexplained (weight decay, SF averaging, schedule?) - hedge.
4. Sec 3 "head barely learned because the output layer moved 1/16 of a small step": at lr 3e-4 the matrices also move ~2e-5 per weight, so blaming the output layer needs evidence; "the matrices and the output layer" is what the arithmetic supports.
5. Sec 1: "whatever the gradient said" overstates - the gradient still sets the singular VECTORS, Muon only flattens the singular values (and Newton-Schulz only approximates this). Also Sec 1 says small output layers go to the second optimizer, while Sec 3 says ours put the 256->1 layer on Muon; one clause in Sec 3 ("which the usual split avoids") would reconcile them.
