CONTINUITY inputs for the Muon guide (round 2). Part A = the original write-up and Moonlight/Muon-is-scalable, read from the arXiv PDF (2502.16982, downloaded today) and Keller Jordan's post. Part B = my own optimizer results, with journal locations. Not yet done: a sweep of my chat/DM logs and tasks file; Part B is journal-only, so treat it as a floor.

A. SOURCES, READ
Keller Jordan's post (kellerjordan.github.io/posts/muon/):
- Muon is for 2D hidden-layer weights; embeddings and the final classifier head go to AdamW ("driven by empirics", the post says the head does not follow from the modular-norm theory), as do scalars/vectors. Conv: flatten the last three dims.
- Newton-Schulz: 5 steps, quintic with coefficients (3.4445, -4.7750, 2.0315), run in bf16. It does NOT return an exact orthogonal matrix: the post picks coefficients so singular values land in about [0.7, 1.3] and says an error of ~0.3 does not hurt the loss curve. (Relevant to our "flat spectrum" wording: it is approximately flat.)
- Original update scale is max(1, rows/cols)^0.5, so 1.0 for square matrices (our lr/16 at width 256 follows).
- Claims: NanoGPT speedrun record 1.35x; a 1.5B model to GPT-2-XL-level HellaSwag in 10 8xH100-hours vs 13.3 h with AdamW; FLOP overhead at most T*m/B (T = NS steps, m = model dim, B = batch in tokens), i.e. small at LM batch sizes (and NOT small at our batch 1-64 and 256-wide heads: worth one sentence, we measured 13-21 ms NS cost).
- The post states as OPEN questions (2024) whether Muon works for fine-tuning or RL. Good to quote: the field has no settled answer, ours is a data point.
Moonlight / Muon is Scalable (Liu et al., Moonshot, 2502.16982), verified in the PDF:
- Two changes were needed to scale Muon: (1) weight decay, (2) a per-matrix update scale. Without decay, weight RMS and layer-output RMS kept growing past the bf16 range and the advantage over AdamW shrank at larger scale; with decay it beat both vanilla Muon and AdamW.
- Lemma 1: for a full-rank [A,B] matrix the Muon update RMS is 1/sqrt(max(A,B)) (they confirm it empirically in training). Adam's update RMS is empirically 0.2-0.4 (theory near 1, but beta1<beta2 and eps>0 keep it below 1). Rule: W <- W - lr*(0.2*O*sqrt(max(A,B)) + wd*W), so Muon "can directly reuse the AdamW lr and wd". They compared this with normalising O to RMS 0.2 and with a per-matrix lr scale, and measured weight RMS on [H,4H] MLP matrices (the shape-dependence is the point).
- Claimed ~2x compute efficiency over AdamW (scaling laws, compute-optimal); Moonlight is 3B/16B MoE, 5.7T tokens. They also released a ZeRO-1 style distributed Muon.
- SFT (Sec 3.5): benefit persists when pretraining AND SFT are both Muon; "when the SFT optimizer differs from the pretraining optimizer, SFT with Muon does not show a significant advantage over AdamW". On Qwen2.5-7B (Adam-pretrained) Muon-SFT was on par with Adam-SFT, and they conclude Muon is better applied at pretraining than at SFT. So Moonlight already reports the mismatch (2605.10468 formalises it) - cite both in section 5.
- Hence: our lr/16 and the 0.2 target are the same two numbers, 1/sqrt(n) and 0.2; section 3's "change of units" is exactly their Lemma 1 + AdamW matching, and that is the citation for it.

